@node Equivalence predicates
@section Equivalence predicates
@nodedescription Procedures that deal with equivalence.

@deffn procedure eqv? @pari{obj} @parii{obj}

The @code{eqv?} procedure defines a useful equivalence relation on
objects.  Briefly, it returns @code{#t} if @vari{obj} and @varii{obj}
are normally regarded as the same object.  This relation is left
slightly open to interpretation, but the following partial
spec@value{shy}ification of @code{eqv?} holds for all implementations of
Scheme.

@lisp
(eqv? 'a 'a)                 @ok{#t}
(eqv? 'a 'b)                 @ok{#f}
(eqv? 2 2)                   @ok{#t}
(eqv? 2 2.0)                 @ok{#f}
(eqv? '() '())               @ok{#t}
(eqv? 100000000 100000000)   @ok{#t}
(eqv? 0.0 +nan.0)            @ok{#f}
(eqv? (cons 1 2) (cons 1 2)) @ok{#f}
(eqv? (lambda () 1)
      (lambda () 2))         @ok{#f}
(let ((p (lambda (x) x)))
  (eqv? p p))                @ok{#t}
(eqv? #f 'nil)               @ok{#f}
@end lisp

The following examples illustrate cases in which the above rules do not
fully specify the behavior of @code{eqv?}. All that can be said about
such cases is that the value returned by @code{eqv?} must be a boolean.

@lisp
(eqv? "" "")          @unspecified{unspecified}
(eqv? '#() '#())      @unspecified{unspecified}
(eqv? (lambda (x) x)  @unspecified{unspecified}
      (lambda (x) x)) @unspecified{unspecified}
(eqv? (lambda (x) x)  @unspecified{unspecified}
      (lambda (y) y)) @unspecified{unspecified}
(eqv? 1.0e0 1.0f0)    @unspecified{unspecified}
(eqv? +nan.0 +nan.0)  @unspecified{unspecified}
@end lisp

Note that @code{(eqv? 0.0 -0.0)} will return @code{#f} if negative zero
is distinguished, and @code{#t} if negative zero is not distinguished.

The next set of examples shows the use of @code{eqv?} with procedures
that have local state.  The @nobr{@code{gen-counter}} procedure must
return a distinct procedure every time, since each procedure has its own
internal counter.  The @nobr{@code{gen-loser}} procedure, however,
returns operationally equivalent procedures each time, since the local
state does not affect the value or side effects of the procedures.
However, @code{eqv?}  may or may not detect this equivalence.

@lisp
(define gen-counter
  (lambda ()
    (let ((n 0))
      (lambda () (set! n (+ n 1)) n))))

(let ((g (gen-counter)))
  (eqv? g g))           @ok{#t}

(eqv? (gen-counter) (gen-counter))
                        @ok{#f}
(define gen-loser
  (lambda ()
    (let ((n 0))
      (lambda () (set! n (+ n 1)) 27))))

(let ((g (gen-loser)))
  (eqv? g g))           @ok{#t}

(eqv? (gen-loser) (gen-loser))
                        @unspecified{unspecified}

(letrec ((f (lambda () (if (eqv? f g) 'both 'f)))
         (g (lambda () (if (eqv? f g) 'both 'g))))
  (eqv? f g))
                        @unspecified{unspecified}

(letrec ((f (lambda () (if (eqv? f g) 'f 'both)))
         (g (lambda () (if (eqv? f g) 'g 'both))))
  (eqv? f g))
                        @ok{#f}
@end lisp

Since it is an error to modify constant objects (those returned by
literal ex@value{shy}pres@value{shy}sions), implementations may share
structure between constants where appropriate.  Thus the value of
@code{eqv?} on constants is sometimes
implemen@value{shy}tation-dependent.

@lisp
(eqv? '(a) '(a))         @unspecified{unspecified}
(eqv? "a" "a")           @unspecified{unspecified}
(eqv? '(b) (cdr '(a b))) @unspecified{unspecified}
(let ((x '(a)))
  (eqv? x x))            @ok{#t}
@end lisp

The above definition of @code{eqv?} allows implementations latitude in
their treat@value{shy}ment of procedures and literals: implementations
may either detect or fail to detect that two procedures or two literals
are equivalent to each other, and can decide whether or not to merge
representations of equivalent objects by using the same pointer or bit
pattern to represent both.

@quotation
@i{Note:} If inexact numbers are represented as @acronym{IEEE} binary
floating-point numbers, then an implementation of @code{eqv?} that
simply com@value{shy}pares equal-sized inexact numbers for bitwise
equality is correct by the above definition.
@end quotation
@end deffn

@deffn procedure eq? @pari{obj} @parii{obj}

The @code{eq?} procedure is similar to @code{eqv?} except that in some
cases it is capable of discerning distinctions finer than those
detectable by @code{eqv?}.  It must al@value{shy}ways return @code{#f}
when @code{eqv?} also would, but may return @code{#f} in some cases
where @code{eqv?} would return @code{#t}.

On symbols, booleans, the empty list, pairs, and records, and also on
non-empty strings, vectors, and bytevectors, @code{eq?} and @code{eqv?}
are guaranteed to have the same behavior.  On procedures, @code{eq?}
must return true if the ar@value{shy}gu@value{shy}ments' location tags
are equal.  On numbers and characters, @code{eq?}'s behavior is
implementation-dependent, but it will always return either true or
false.  On empty strings, empty vectors, and empty bytevectors,
@code{eq?} may also be@value{shy}have differently from @code{eqv?}.

@lisp
(eq? 'a 'a)               @ok{#t}
(eq? '(a) '(a))           @unspecified{unspecified}
(eq? (list 'a) (list 'a)) @ok{#f}
(eq? "a" "a")             @unspecified{unspecified}
(eq? "" "")               @unspecified{unspecified}
(eq? '() '())             @ok{#t}
(eq? 2 2)                 @unspecified{unspecified}
(eq? #\A #\A)             @unspecified{unspecified}
(eq? car car)             @ok{#t}
(let ((n (+ 2 3)))
  (eq? n n))              @unspecified{unspecified}
(let ((x '(a)))
  (eq? x x))              @ok{#t}
(let ((x '#()))
  (eq? x x))              @ok{#t}
(let ((p (lambda (x) x)))
  (eq? p p))              @ok{#t}
@end lisp

@quotation
It will usually be possible to implement @code{eq?} much more
efficiently than @code{eqv?}, for example, as a simple pointer
comparison instead of as some more complicated operation.  One reason is
that it is not al@value{shy}ways possible to compute @code{eqv?} of two
numbers in constant time, whereas @code{eq?} implemented as pointer
comparison will always finish in constant time.
@end quotation
@end deffn

@deffn procedure equal? @pari{obj} @parii{obj}

The @code{equal?} procedure, when applied to pairs, vectors, strings and
byte@value{shy}vectors, recursively compares them, returning @code{#t}
when the unfoldings of its arguments into (possibly infinite) trees are
equal (in the sense of @code{equal?}) as ordered trees, and @code{#f}
otherwise.  It returns the same as @code{eqv?} when applied to booleans,
symbols, numbers, characters, ports, procedures, and the
emp@value{shy}ty list.  If two objects are @code{eqv?}, they must be
@code{equal?} as well.  In all other cases, @code{equal?} may return
either @code{#t} or @code{#f}.  Even if its arguments are circular data
structures, @code{equal?} must always terminate.

@lisp
(equal? 'a 'a)               @ok{#t}
(equal? '(a) '(a))           @ok{#t}
(equal? '(a (b) c)
        '(a (b) c))          @ok{#t}
(equal? "abc" "abc")         @ok{#t}
(equal? 2 2)                 @ok{#t}
(equal? (make-vector 5 'a)
        (make-vector 5 'a))  @ok{#t}
(equal? '#1=(a b . #1#)
        '#2=(a b a b . #2#)) @ok{#t}
(equal? (lambda (x) x)
        (lambda (y) y))      @unspecified{unspecified}
@end lisp

@quotation
@i{Note:} A rule of thumb is that objects are generally @code{equal?} if
they print the same.
@end quotation
@end deffn
